{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-39749" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{x}^{3}-2$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065132/state.json" "file_sha256" "daec3c2ec1201783d4e544f0820742e2f4191af958911348f8ca75ee46fdb04f"} {"question" "q-39749" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{t}^{3}-2⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,-1⟩, \\\\ r(\\pi ) & =⟨\\pi ,{\\pi }^{3}-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,-1)$ and $(\\pi ,{\\pi }^{3}-3).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(\\pi ))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(\\pi ,{\\pi }^{3}-3)-f(0,0) \\\\  & ={\\pi }^{2}\\cdot ({\\pi }^{3}-3)-{0}^{2}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & ={\\pi }^{2}({\\pi }^{3}-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065132/state.json" "file_sha256" "daec3c2ec1201783d4e544f0820742e2f4191af958911348f8ca75ee46fdb04f"} {"question" "q-39749" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065132/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c5080a52794785add16c8ed18b74da5acbf40b57db4969ac0f2d0994b12fc0af"} {"question" "q-39749" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065132/state.json" "file_sha256" "daec3c2ec1201783d4e544f0820742e2f4191af958911348f8ca75ee46fdb04f"} {"question" "q-39749" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\pi ({\\pi }^{3}-2)"] ["math" "{\\pi }^{2}({\\pi }^{2}-3)"] ["math" "{\\pi }^{2}({\\pi }^{3}-1)"] ["math" "{\\pi }^{2}({\\pi }^{3}-3)"] ["math" "{\\pi }^{5}-1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065132/state.json" "file_sha256" "daec3c2ec1201783d4e544f0820742e2f4191af958911348f8ca75ee46fdb04f"} {"question" "q-39749" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\pi }^{2}({\\pi }^{3}-3)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065132/state.json" "file_sha256" "daec3c2ec1201783d4e544f0820742e2f4191af958911348f8ca75ee46fdb04f"} {"question" "q-98995" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the curve $C,$ defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi t}{2}),t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi t}{2})⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ for $F=x{y}^{2}\\,i+{x}^{2}y\\,j.$ \n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065132/state.json" "file_sha256" "daec3c2ec1201783d4e544f0820742e2f4191af958911348f8ca75ee46fdb04f"} {"question" "q-98995" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f.$ Since we're given that $F$ is conservative, we know that such a function exists. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =x{y}^{2}, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & ={x}^{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. The position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,1⟩, \\\\ r(1) & =⟨2,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,1)$ and $(2,1).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(2,1)-f(0,1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {2}^{2}\\cdot {1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {0}^{2}\\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\  & =2-0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065132/state.json" "file_sha256" "daec3c2ec1201783d4e544f0820742e2f4191af958911348f8ca75ee46fdb04f"} {"question" "q-98995" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065132/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c5080a52794785add16c8ed18b74da5acbf40b57db4969ac0f2d0994b12fc0af"} {"question" "q-98995" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065132/state.json" "file_sha256" "daec3c2ec1201783d4e544f0820742e2f4191af958911348f8ca75ee46fdb04f"} {"question" "q-98995" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065132/state.json" "file_sha256" "daec3c2ec1201783d4e544f0820742e2f4191af958911348f8ca75ee46fdb04f"} {"question" "q-98995" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065132/state.json" "file_sha256" "daec3c2ec1201783d4e544f0820742e2f4191af958911348f8ca75ee46fdb04f"} {"question" "q-104625" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $f(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi y}{2}).$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $y={x}^{3}+1$ for $x\\in [1,2],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065132/state.json" "file_sha256" "daec3c2ec1201783d4e544f0820742e2f4191af958911348f8ca75ee46fdb04f"} {"question" "q-104625" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,{t}^{3}+1⟩,\\,t\\in [1,2].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨1,2⟩, \\\\ r(2) & =⟨2,9⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(1,2)$ and $(2,9).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(2))-f(r(1)) \\\\  & =f(2,9)-f(1,2) \\\\  & ={e}^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{9\\pi }{2})-{e}^{1}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & ={e}^{2}\\cdot 1-{e}^{1}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & ={e}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065132/state.json" "file_sha256" "daec3c2ec1201783d4e544f0820742e2f4191af958911348f8ca75ee46fdb04f"} {"question" "q-104625" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065132/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c5080a52794785add16c8ed18b74da5acbf40b57db4969ac0f2d0994b12fc0af"} {"question" "q-104625" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065132/state.json" "file_sha256" "daec3c2ec1201783d4e544f0820742e2f4191af958911348f8ca75ee46fdb04f"} {"question" "q-104625" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "1+e"] ["math" "e-{e}^{4}"] ["math" "{e}^{2}"] ["math" "{e}^{2}-e"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065132/state.json" "file_sha256" "daec3c2ec1201783d4e544f0820742e2f4191af958911348f8ca75ee46fdb04f"} {"question" "q-104625" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{e}^{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065132/state.json" "file_sha256" "daec3c2ec1201783d4e544f0820742e2f4191af958911348f8ca75ee46fdb04f"} {"question" "q-104731" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the curve $C,$ defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2t,{t}^{2}-1⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 2.\n$$\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ for $F=(2xy-y)\\,i+({x}^{2}-x)\\,j.$ \n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065132/state.json" "file_sha256" "daec3c2ec1201783d4e544f0820742e2f4191af958911348f8ca75ee46fdb04f"} {"question" "q-104731" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f.$ Since we're given that $F$ is conservative, we know that such a function exists. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =2xy-y, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & ={x}^{2}-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{2}y-xy.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. The position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,-1⟩, \\\\ r(2) & =⟨4,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,-1)$ and $(4,3).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(4,3)-f(0,-1) \\\\  & =({4}^{2}\\cdot 3-4\\cdot 3)-({0}^{2}\\cdot (-1)-0\\cdot (-1)) \\\\  & =48-12 \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065132/state.json" "file_sha256" "daec3c2ec1201783d4e544f0820742e2f4191af958911348f8ca75ee46fdb04f"} {"question" "q-104731" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065132/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c5080a52794785add16c8ed18b74da5acbf40b57db4969ac0f2d0994b12fc0af"} {"question" "q-104731" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065132/state.json" "file_sha256" "daec3c2ec1201783d4e544f0820742e2f4191af958911348f8ca75ee46fdb04f"} {"question" "q-104731" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "12"] ["math" "24"] ["math" "36"] ["math" "48"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065132/state.json" "file_sha256" "daec3c2ec1201783d4e544f0820742e2f4191af958911348f8ca75ee46fdb04f"} {"question" "q-104731" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "36"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065132/state.json" "file_sha256" "daec3c2ec1201783d4e544f0820742e2f4191af958911348f8ca75ee46fdb04f"} {"question" "q-124034" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $2x=\\pi {y}^{3}$ for $y\\in [0,1],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065132/state.json" "file_sha256" "daec3c2ec1201783d4e544f0820742e2f4191af958911348f8ca75ee46fdb04f"} {"question" "q-124034" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\frac{\\pi {t}^{3}}{2},t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩, \\\\ r(1) & =⟨\\frac{\\pi }{2},1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(1))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},1)-f(0,0) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0\\operatorname{cos}⁡0 \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡1-1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065132/state.json" "file_sha256" "daec3c2ec1201783d4e544f0820742e2f4191af958911348f8ca75ee46fdb04f"} {"question" "q-124034" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065132/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c5080a52794785add16c8ed18b74da5acbf40b57db4969ac0f2d0994b12fc0af"} {"question" "q-124034" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065132/state.json" "file_sha256" "daec3c2ec1201783d4e544f0820742e2f4191af958911348f8ca75ee46fdb04f"} {"question" "q-124034" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "1+\\pi "] ["math" "1-\\frac{\\pi }{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{2}+1"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{2}-1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065132/state.json" "file_sha256" "daec3c2ec1201783d4e544f0820742e2f4191af958911348f8ca75ee46fdb04f"} {"question" "q-124034" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065132/state.json" "file_sha256" "daec3c2ec1201783d4e544f0820742e2f4191af958911348f8ca75ee46fdb04f"}] "usage" {"q-39749" [] "q-98995" []} "basis" 2134 "retractions" [[17592186134161 :question/problem "Let $f(x, y) = x^{2}y$. Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $y = \\cos x + x^{3} - 2$ for $x ∈ [0, π]$, evaluate $∫_{C}∇f \\cdot dr$."] [17592186134486 :question/problem "Consider the curve $C$, defined parametrically as\n\n$r(t) = ⟨t + \\sin (\\frac{πt